{
  "id": "2026-10-16-ads-spyware",
  "title": "Researchers say an ad company was quietly delivering spyware through ads",
  "topic": "테크",
  "level": 3,
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
  "sentences": [
    "Good morning!",
    "Today's story is about something most of us scroll past every day: online ads.",
    "Security researchers at Citizen Lab say an Israeli company called Discover-Tech posed as a normal ad firm.",
    "But behind the scenes, it was helping spyware makers reach specific people.",
    "Here's how it worked.",
    "When one of its ads loaded on a phone, a small script checked who the user was.",
    "For almost everyone, nothing happened.",
    "But if the device was on a target list, the ad quietly ran malicious code.",
    "The victim didn't have to click anything at all.",
    "The researchers say at least forty-five devices at Al Jazeera were targeted this way.",
    "They also went through almost four billion ad auctions.",
    "Discover-Tech's ads showed up on around two hundred thousand apps and websites.",
    "Some of them belonged to governments, the UN, and NATO.",
    "After the report came out, Google and several ad networks cut the company off.",
    "The bigger lesson is uncomfortable.",
    "The same tools that let marketers find the right customer can also be turned against one person.",
    "So the next time an ad seems to know you a little too well, it might be worth a second thought.",
    "Have a great Friday!"
  ],
  "summary_ko": [
    "시티즌랩이 이스라엘 광고 회사 Discover-Tech 가 평범한 광고 회사로 위장해 스파이웨어 업체의 표적 공격을 도왔다고 발표했다.",
    "광고가 뜰 때 스크립트가 기기를 확인하고, 표적이면 클릭 없이도 악성 코드를 실행했다 — 알자지라 기기 최소 45대가 표적이 됐다.",
    "약 40억 건의 광고 입찰을 분석한 결과 20만 개 앱·사이트에 노출됐고, 발표 후 구글 등이 이 회사를 차단했다."
  ],
